A||N||23||Motorhead||Suspension Specifics?||||5||
Z||000000||Motorhead||02-29-2000||05:22 AM||||<BR>The recent demise of a front wheel <BR>bearing has prompted a complete <BR>front suspension inspection.  <BR>Naturally,this in turn prompted a <BR>complete front end rebuild.  Which <BR>has now snowballed into a complete <BR>suspension upgrade, front and <BR>rear!  Funny how that works!<P>So now with the front end parts <BR>spread on the floor before me, I'm <BR>looking for suggestions.<P>What is: best value in poly <BR>bushings, lowering springs, <BR>shocks, sway bars ,and brake <BR>upgrades.  Are dropped spindles <BR>worth the expense?<P>This car is an '86 coupe, and will <BR>be a frequent driver, with some <BR>autocrossing.  I plan on using 15" <BR>GT wheels, and want to lower the <BR>car without turning the ride <BR>quality into that of a dump truck.<P>I figure all y'all have tried just <BR>about everything out there.<P>Anybody got the "Bumt Steer Kit" <BR>yet?<P>Thanks||||reg||5
Z||000001||Monkeyman||02-29-2000||05:49 AM||||I don't know about much of this but I'll tell you what I've heard.  If you go with lowering/sport springs use the Suspension Technologies ones from JCWhitney.  Addco sway bars from the same place.  Go with the Grand Am brake upgrade unless you really need the E-brake.  (You'll lose the E-brake with that upgrade.)  And good luck finding dropped spindles.  Doug Kinney had to pay $350 for his used (I assume) ones off of eBay if I remember his story correctly.  There are a couple of places that advertise them but don't sell them anymore.  Personally, I think that would be the way to go if you can find any, but then I don't know what you'd do to stiffen your suspension.||||reg||1
Z||000002||batboy||02-29-2000||03:19 PM||||I've done one of those front end rebuilds that snowballed into a major project too. I used Moog brand suspension and steering replacement parts (tie rods, ball joints, etc.). Prothane bushings appear to be the best bang for your buck as far as poly goes. If your budget can afford it, coil-overs front and back are the best route to go. You can easily pick and choose what spring rate to use and you can adjust your ride height up or down whenever you want. I just ordered the RCC bump steer kit, but I haven't installed it yet. By the way, I called Monday (2-27-00) and found out that JC Whitney is back ordered on the Addco rear sway bars until late March.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Motorhead||02-29-2000||06:01 PM||||I'm afraid coil-overs are out of the question, too expensve, maybe on the '88 GT I dream about at night!<P>This car could become a daily driver.  I want something to keep the cobwebs out, with some handling for brushing up the old autocross bug that keeps coming back to haunt me.<P>Don't mind spending the bucks on Konis' and oversize bars, slightly stiffer springs, and lower C.G., but don't need a second mortgage on the house.<P>I'm looking to enjoy the ride, and play on the weekends,"on a budget".<P>This little car is allready fun. compared to the Triumphs', MGs', and Sunbeams I've played with, it's pure joy!<P>Who's autocrossing this car with success, and what kind of setups are you running?<P>Thanks!||||reg||5
Z||000004||batboy||02-29-2000||08:20 PM||||If the coil-overs are out because they're too expensive, then the Konis are probably out too. That's ok, you can still have a good suspension setup without a lot of expense, that's the nice thing about Fieros.<P>Lower and stiffer springs? Really there's only two choices right now. Eibachs and Suspension Techniques. Which is best? Don't know! I have the S/T springs in front, but don't know how they compare to the Eibachs. I heard the Eibachs may give a slighter smoother ride and lowers the front about 1 to 1-1/2 inches compared to the S/T springs which lowers the front about 1-1/2 to 2 inches. The S/T's are cheaper especially if bought at JC Whitney.<P>Poly bushings help tighten up the ride, along with new shocks and struts. The Fiero Store offers a KYB performance kit that includes a rear sway bar and KYB performance shocks and struts. Lots of choices out there. Hope this gives you some ideas to start out with.||||reg||1
Z||000005||batboy||02-29-2000||08:24 PM||||Tires! I forgot about tires. Probably the single thing you can do to improve handling the most, is to buy a good set of performance tires.||||reg||1
Z||000006||1FST2M6||02-29-2000||09:04 PM||||I have KONIs and Eibachs. All the Poly bushings you can shake a stick at. On my current tires with a driver that has a clue (not myself) at road atlanta my car will pull  1.12-13g on a 200' skid pad in both directions. (i'm not a very smoother driver) I got my konis and springs from <A HREF="http://www.shox.com" TARGET=_blank>www.shox.com</A>  the whole set for $618. As for tires running Yokohama A509s on my 14s if your gunna have 15s a better tire at about the same price is the Yokohama AVS intermediate. well crafted, and STICKY!!!!  Have a set on an Alfa Romeo 164L (4 door) and their quiet and grippy at any temperature. No real need for heat cycling them just mount them and ride! And before you spend a fortune buying those budhings from The fieros store call Nopi... take the first digit off The fiero Stores part number and that is Prothanes number give that to a sales rep at NOPI and save about 50% My cradle mount set was only $45 with shipping.. not $69 + shipping<p>[This message has been edited by 1FST2M6 (edited 02-29-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||Motorhead||02-29-2000||11:14 PM||||Thanks ya'll!! <P>The Eibachs sound like the ticket.<P>Currently running Yokohama on the Z-Car, great tires!<P>Konis aren't out of the question.  Ms Motorhead hasn't reached the limit yet!  <P>What about this rack?  Only steering stabilizer I see is Monroe!  Any improvements there?<P>Mine has left a big puddle on the garage floor.  Bushings For the rack?<P>Anybody running strut tower braces?||||reg||1
Z||000008||JMergen||03-01-2000||12:28 AM||||Just finished rebuilding the suspension on my 86 SE. I used the Fiero kit from SUSPENSION RETORATION (www.suspension.com) everything but the inboard tierod ends for$340. + $9.05 s&h. Cant see how anybody would want a stiffer ride with out a kidney belt. Hope it softens up a bit as it brakes in.||||reg||1
Z||000009||batboy||03-01-2000||07:12 AM||||I used a Moog brand steering stabilizer. if you notice a little play in the rack (where the inner tie rod goes in, Rodney Dickman offers a replacement aluminum bushing rather than the plastic factory one. If the rack is more worn out than that, it must be changed.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Motorhead||03-01-2000||04:05 PM||||Thanks for the great parts sources y'all.  Both Energy Suspension and shox.com seem to have some great prices. <P>1FST2M6, those are some pretty impressive skid pad numbers from just a couple of mods.  Is it true the  Fiero would pull close to 1G in stock form?  Heard that somewhere. ||||reg||1
Z||000011||Motorhead||03-01-2000||04:09 PM||||batboy, think I'll try the rack bushing you mentioned.  Steering isn't quite as tight as I think it should be.  Rack seems to be in good condition though.||||reg||1
Z||000012||Eric||03-02-2000||04:57 PM||||You can't get A509s anymore.||||reg||1
Z||000013||1FST2M6||03-02-2000||05:19 PM||||I got my A509s in June and have only put about 2600 miles on them.. but the rear are nearly bald.. Yeah i've heard the same about it pullin a full g in stock trim.. I'm sure with a set of Yokohama A032 you could with out really trying.. The shop i deal with uses those (A032s) on ALL their SCCA (IT-A, IT-C, HSR, and SVRA cars) Like i said though... I didn't pull those numbers!!! a skilled driver with a driving ability WAY above min did... the bes i could do was .98 and them the back tires would over power... I'm not a very smooth driver... Lucky for me drag racing doesn't require smoothness... I can pull well over a g on acceleration thought no problem!... Oh a similar tire to the A509 is the Yokohama AVS intermediate!!! thier actually better than the 509s... they can be found at  <A HREF="http://www.discounttiredirect.com" TARGET=_blank>www.discounttiredirect.com</A>  and their actually pretty inexpensive!! oh and the strut tower bar you asked about.. not nessary for the front but the rear is available at.. <A HREF="http://home.earthlink.net/~chriswest/" TARGET=_blank>http://home.earthlink.net/~chriswest/</A> <p>[This message has been edited by 1FST2M6 (edited 03-02-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000014||BBBAD GT||03-02-2000||06:17 PM||||I'm following this post pretty closely, because I'm probably going to do the suspension soon enough.  My question is how much do these "lowering" springs lower the car (eibach and st)?  I think maybe since my GT is 13 yrs old, the stock springs have just compressed enough to look like I have a lower car.  Do other people find this to be true?  If I lower the car anymore, I'll be the next snow-plow in town.  My new wheels/tires should be the same height as the stockers in the pic, but we'll see.<P> <IMG SRC="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/Fiero_files/image010.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000015||Motorhead||03-02-2000||11:32 PM||||When you polish the sides of that beer can you straddled, laying on its side, I guess your low enough.<P>Had to call a tow truck when my wife got the MG stuck in a driveway with all four wheels off the ground!<P>Keep the snow shovel handy for clearing the nose of the car!||||reg||1
Z||000016||Motorhead||03-02-2000||11:43 PM||||I don't think any of my other rides could do 1G!  Not in stock form.<P>Now, if I could get rid of the bump steer, I'd have a near wonderful handling car.||||reg||1
Z||000017||Monkeyman||03-03-2000||07:04 AM||||BBBBBBBBBBBBBBAD GT--Your car looks stock.  This pic shows a lowered car.  (Sorry, I forget whose car it is.)<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/86se99a_1.jpg">  <BR><p>[This message has been edited by Monkeyman (edited 03-04-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000018||Big O||03-03-2000||09:41 AM||||I went the Eibach-KYB-polyurethane route on my 86 GTP when the new 3.8 Series II SC power plant went in.  I also went to 16" tires.  Yes, the car really cornered great. Quick and flat in the corners.  The only problem was that the ride was so harsh you could hardly stand to be in the car on anything but a completely smooth freeway, and where do you find those anymore.  The harsh ride eventually (after about 6,000 miles) knocked the front end out of line, damaged a lower ball joint and the rack and pinion.  This is a great setup if you want to use your car for an SCCA toy, but you probably will do the same thing I did (remove the Eibach springs) if you are going to use it as a commuter scooter.  The Eibachs lowered the car about 1 1/2" which did make it look nice, but it just wasn't worth the grief.||||reg||6
Z||000019||BBBAD GT||03-03-2000||09:43 AM||||MM:  Do you happen to know what kind of springs are on that car above?  That looks pretty good, as its not 'too' lowered to have the fenders almost touch the tires.  Ever see the Fiero on the cover of the 2000 Fiero Store catalog?  It looks like its ready to go 4X4ing.  I just assumed that pic was stock, and my slightly lowered ride was the result of 13 yrs of gravity winning the battle.<P> <A HREF="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/Fiero.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/Fiero.htm</A> <P>||||reg||1
Z||000020||GREENDANO||03-03-2000||08:17 PM||||Monkeyman,<BR>  That unknown car is John Altman's 86 SE.  This is the dude that I traded my GT for his F40 kit.  I saw this car in person, and, as always with Fieros, the pictures don't do it justice.  Nice car....  Here's a link to his sight<BR>--&gt; <A HREF="http://home.neo.rr.com/fieros/" TARGET=_blank>http://home.neo.rr.com/fieros/</A> <BR>Check out his SC 3800 swap link.  He's doing the swap right now.  I just sent him some parts this week.  He tells me the swap is coming along quite well.<BR>  Check it out.  Also, check out the 85 GT with the PISA fastback.  Look familiar?....  I miss the old girl.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><BR>  Dan-O||||reg||1
Z||000021||lowCG||03-04-2000||02:54 PM||||The ride height on the Yellow car is right where I'm at with my car,seems to be as far as you can go without screwing up the geometry(function)of the suspension.Bridgestone RE71s are also a good alternative to A509s,pretty much the same price/performance.All my suspension mods involve using off the shelf parts from several other cars,which makes it a hassle compared to one box of springs,but I'm picky.||||reg||1
Z||000022||Monkeyman||03-04-2000||04:45 PM||||Thanx, Dan-O.<BR>Ya'll remember what John Altmans car looks like 'cause that's what mine will look like in a couple of years (except for the paint and the wheels).<BR>For those of you unfamiliar with Dan-O GT, here it is.  It's an '85 GT with PISAs GTfx fastback conversion kit (correct me if I'm wrong).<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/'85GT_GTFxconversion.jpg">  <p>[This message has been edited by Monkeyman (edited 03-04-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000023||Eric||03-04-2000||04:52 PM||||I just had the last available set in the country of 225/60/15 A509s installed on my rear wheels today.  They wear like crazy, but they do handle well.  The tread wear rating is 160 same as the AVS intermediates.  I think the AVS' have more chunky rubber on them and are V and Z speed rated.||||reg||1
